 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>At Milan’s Aquarium, with its leafy garden and filtered summer light, Luisa Beccaria’s Herbarium felt less like a theme than a book come to life. The collection opened like an imaginary botanical archive, with its flowers translated into color, texture, and movement. Petal-like ruffles, cutwork, appliqué, and embroidered blooms gave the clothes a sense of unfurling, while luminous organzas, liquid-looking fabrics, and sequins caught and reflected the light.</p><p>There was a pleasing dimensionality to the weightless prettiness: Dégradé mohair knits shifted from one color to another like evening skies or water, while poplins, brocades, stretch cottons, and graphic stripes provided a more grounded counterpoint. Even the swimwear was folded into the same decorative language, with retro-tinged silhouettes and matching little skirts.</p><p>The palette ventured beyond the expected garden pastels into vivid violet, watery blue, and warm brown. Lace, crochet effects, and intricate handiwork supplied the artisanal detail beneath the romance. Beccaria has always understood the pleasure of dressing up; here the pleasure was in making those clothes move, breathe, and catch the light along with the garden around them.</p></div>",
